                <pb facs="0167" n="163" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg163"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94119/418: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-03T12:00:00+08:00">May 3, 1938—noon.</date><lb/>[Received
                        May 3—8:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>232. Department’s 124, April 13, 7 p.m.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d158-f1"/>
                    Counselor [of] British Embassy informed me today in the course of a conversation
                    that question of mediation had been recently canvassed by the British
                    Government, but that conclusion had been reached that mediation was out of the
                    question as there was no basis upon which mediation could be profitably offered
                    to either side.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
